WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.986
- Don't sleepwalk through
life, and don't say

00:00:02.986 --> 00:00:04.536
it's all going to be great.

00:00:04.536 --> 00:00:07.028
I'll do this and I'll do that.

00:00:07.028 --> 00:00:08.893
I'm just marking time to be over.

00:00:08.893 --> 00:00:10.859
I've told people that's like saving up sex

00:00:10.859 --> 00:00:12.005
for your old age.

00:00:12.005 --> 00:00:14.866
I don't have to do, follow what

00:00:14.866 --> 00:00:17.959
Wallstreet is telling me
to do in that quarter.

00:00:17.959 --> 00:00:20.136
So I own the brush, I own the canvas,

00:00:20.136 --> 00:00:21.318
and the canvas is unlimited.

00:00:21.318 --> 00:00:24.110
Defining your circle of competence is

00:00:24.110 --> 00:00:26.401
the most important aspect of investing.

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:28.241
Bill says if I'd been born
a few thousand years ago,

00:00:28.241 --> 00:00:29.657
I'd have been some animals lunch,

00:00:29.657 --> 00:00:33.424
because I'd have gone on
saying, well I allocate capital.

00:00:33.424 --> 00:00:34.940
And the animal would say,
they're the kind that

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.664
taste the best.

00:00:36.664 --> 00:00:39.035
And I can't run fast
and I can't climb trees.

00:00:39.035 --> 00:00:41.112
- He's been the chairman
and largest shareholder

00:00:41.112 --> 00:00:44.002
of Berkshire Hathaway since 1970.

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:46.215
His success in investing
has lead to people

00:00:46.215 --> 00:00:49.612
calling him the Oracle or Sage of Omaha.

00:00:49.612 --> 00:00:52.074
He's considered to be the
most successful investor

00:00:52.074 --> 00:00:54.713
in the world and is the
second wealthiest person

00:00:54.713 --> 00:00:56.674
in the world with an estimated net worth

00:00:56.674 --> 00:00:58.091
of $73.9 billion.

00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:01.482
He's Warren Buffet, and here is my take

00:01:01.482 --> 00:01:04.648
on his top ten rules
to success volume two.

00:01:04.648 --> 00:01:06.129
Rule number is my personal favorite,

00:01:06.129 --> 00:01:07.791
and make sure to stick
around all the way to the end

00:01:07.791 --> 00:01:09.335
for some special bonus clips.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:11.290
Also, as Warren is talking,
if he says something

00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:12.814
that is really meaningful to you,

00:01:12.814 --> 00:01:14.211
please leave it in the comments below.

00:01:14.211 --> 00:01:15.809
Put quotes around it
so other people can be

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:17.359
inspired as well.

00:01:17.359 --> 00:01:18.192
Enjoy.

00:01:21.227 --> 00:01:24.144
(uplifting jingle)

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.224
- I was just wondering
what would you consider

00:01:33.224 --> 00:01:36.857
to be the worst investment
you've ever made?

00:01:36.857 --> 00:01:38.690
- The worst investment I ever made?

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:42.361
How long do you have?
(audience laughs)

00:01:42.361 --> 00:01:44.348
I made some very bad
ones, but that doesn't

00:01:44.348 --> 00:01:46.241
really bother me.

00:01:46.241 --> 00:01:48.592
It may bother the shareholders,
but that's another question.

00:01:48.592 --> 00:01:51.083
(audience laughing)

00:01:51.083 --> 00:01:53.016
You're going to make mistakes in life.

00:01:53.016 --> 00:01:54.656
I mean, there's no question about it.

00:01:54.656 --> 00:01:56.320
You don't want to make
them on the big decisions,

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.310
you know, who you marry
and some things like that.

00:01:58.310 --> 00:02:00.593
So there's no way I'm
going to make a lot of

00:02:00.593 --> 00:02:02.147
business and investment decisions without

00:02:02.147 --> 00:02:03.101
making some mistakes.

00:02:03.101 --> 00:02:05.159
I may try to minimize them.

00:02:05.159 --> 00:02:08.509
I don't dwell on them at
all. I don't look back.

00:02:08.509 --> 00:02:12.189
The biggest mistakes are
the ones that actually

00:02:12.189 --> 00:02:13.498
don't show up.

00:02:13.498 --> 00:02:15.722
They're the mistakes of
omission rather than commission.

00:02:15.722 --> 00:02:19.889
We've never lost that much
money on any one investment.

00:02:21.044 --> 00:02:22.933
But it's the things
that I knew enough to do

00:02:22.933 --> 00:02:23.766
that I didn't do.

00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:27.933
We have missed profits of
as much as maybe $10 billion

00:02:29.238 --> 00:02:31.989
in things that I knew enough
to do and I didn't do.

00:02:31.989 --> 00:02:35.364
Now the fact I didn't
buy Microsoft way back

00:02:35.364 --> 00:02:37.804
is not a foregone
opportunity because I didn't

00:02:37.804 --> 00:02:39.545
know enough to make that decision.

00:02:39.545 --> 00:02:42.791
But there have been other investments

00:02:42.791 --> 00:02:45.067
where I did know enough
to make the decision,

00:02:45.067 --> 00:02:47.827
and for one reason or another,
I either didn't do it at all

00:02:47.827 --> 00:02:49.590
or I did it on a small scale.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.192
I was sucking my thumb
when I should've been

00:02:51.192 --> 00:02:53.307
writing checks basically.
(chuckles)

00:02:53.307 --> 00:02:55.576
And those don't show up.

00:02:55.576 --> 00:02:58.084
There's no place where it
shows missed opportunities,

00:02:58.084 --> 00:03:00.326
but I've missed some big ones.

00:03:00.326 --> 00:03:03.864
The triumphs in life are partly triumphs

00:03:03.864 --> 00:03:05.144
because you know that everything isn't

00:03:05.144 --> 00:03:06.509
going to be a triumph.

00:03:06.509 --> 00:03:10.588
And I would never get
too hung up on mistakes.

00:03:10.588 --> 00:03:12.826
I know a lot of people that
really agonize over them.

00:03:12.826 --> 00:03:14.465
And it just isn't worth.

00:03:14.465 --> 00:03:16.599
I mean, tomorrow's another day
and you'll live it forward,

00:03:16.599 --> 00:03:18.109
and just go on to the next thing.

00:03:18.109 --> 00:03:21.824
I advise students, as much as possible,

00:03:21.824 --> 00:03:23.720
look for the job that you would take if

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:25.107
you didn't need a job.

00:03:25.107 --> 00:03:27.128
I mean, don't sleepwalk through life,

00:03:27.128 --> 00:03:30.419
and don't say it's all going to be great.

00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:32.244
I'll do this and I'll do that.

00:03:32.244 --> 00:03:34.283
I'm just marking time to be over.

00:03:34.283 --> 00:03:36.308
I've told people, that's
like saving up sex

00:03:36.308 --> 00:03:37.720
for your old age.

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:39.824
I mean, it just is not...
(audience laughing)

00:03:39.824 --> 00:03:41.657
It is not a good idea.

00:03:43.984 --> 00:03:47.151
(audience applauding)

00:03:50.007 --> 00:03:52.027
- [Charlie] What are
you urging them to do?

00:03:52.027 --> 00:03:55.011
- I'll explain...
(audience laughing)

00:03:55.011 --> 00:03:57.344
Just what I'm talking about.

00:03:58.545 --> 00:03:59.378
Now if...

00:04:02.352 --> 00:04:05.615
You really want to be
doing what you love doing.

00:04:05.615 --> 00:04:08.395
And you can't necessarily
find it on your first job.

00:04:08.395 --> 00:04:10.834
But don't give up before you find it.

00:04:10.834 --> 00:04:13.695
- What brings you the most satisfaction,

00:04:13.695 --> 00:04:14.836
beyond family?

00:04:14.836 --> 00:04:15.669
- Well...

00:04:17.755 --> 00:04:20.161
Greatest satisfaction is
just staying in good health.

00:04:20.161 --> 00:04:22.240
I mean, when you're 86, I mean,

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.869
you look at this a little
differently. (chuckles)

00:04:24.869 --> 00:04:26.708
- [Charlie] So you're in good health?

00:04:26.708 --> 00:04:28.920
- Oh yeah, yeah. I mean, I enjoy everyday.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:29.816
I enjoy--

00:04:29.816 --> 00:04:31.507
- [Charlie] But what is it that you enjoy?

00:04:31.507 --> 00:04:33.747
- Well I enjoy running Berkshire.

00:04:33.747 --> 00:04:36.233
I mean, if you get
right down to my psyche.

00:04:36.233 --> 00:04:37.327
- [Charlie] That's what
I want to (mumbles).

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:41.511
- Yeah, no, it's been my
painting for 50-some years.

00:04:41.511 --> 00:04:42.586
I get to paint what I want.

00:04:42.586 --> 00:04:45.169
I don't have to do, follow what

00:04:46.564 --> 00:04:48.532
Wallstreet is telling
me to do in that quarter

00:04:48.532 --> 00:04:49.550
or something like that.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.787
So I own the brush, I own the canvas,

00:04:51.787 --> 00:04:53.531
and the canvas is unlimited.

00:04:53.531 --> 00:04:55.131
Now that's a pretty nice game,

00:04:55.131 --> 00:04:58.635
and I get to do it everyday
with people I like.

00:04:58.635 --> 00:05:00.762
I don't have to associate with anyone

00:05:00.762 --> 00:05:03.247
that causes my stomach to churn.

00:05:03.247 --> 00:05:04.820
If I were in politics, I'd have to smile

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.444
at a lot of people that
I want to hit, you know.

00:05:08.444 --> 00:05:09.835
- [Charlie] You just don't talk to them.

00:05:09.835 --> 00:05:11.862
- So it's really, I've got a good deal,

00:05:11.862 --> 00:05:14.906
and I'm hanging on to it. (chuckles)

00:05:14.906 --> 00:05:16.980
- I'd like to know, we all consider you

00:05:16.980 --> 00:05:19.444
to be the financial business experts,

00:05:19.444 --> 00:05:21.746
and we look to you and follow your lead.

00:05:21.746 --> 00:05:23.755
But when you need advise and feedback

00:05:23.755 --> 00:05:26.039
about an idea or
decision, I'd like to know

00:05:26.039 --> 00:05:27.456
who do you go to.

00:05:28.656 --> 00:05:30.095
- Well usually I look in the mirror.

00:05:30.095 --> 00:05:33.676
(audience laughing)

00:05:33.676 --> 00:05:36.843
(audience applauding)

00:05:42.056 --> 00:05:44.517
The nature of what I do

00:05:44.517 --> 00:05:48.099
means I have to think
pretty much independently.

00:05:48.099 --> 00:05:51.060
Because if I take a poll, in effect,

00:05:51.060 --> 00:05:52.460
I'm going to do what
everybody else is doing,

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.877
and I don't think much of
that usually in investments.

00:05:54.877 --> 00:05:58.615
And so I have to have an environment,

00:05:58.615 --> 00:06:01.010
I have to have a temperament personally,

00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:03.119
that lets me think for myself.

00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:05.786
And I've got a terrific partner,

00:06:06.749 --> 00:06:08.532
fella named Charlie Monger.

00:06:08.532 --> 00:06:11.449
And you can't find anybody smarter,

00:06:12.787 --> 00:06:14.724
any better quality of anything.

00:06:14.724 --> 00:06:16.947
But we think a lot alike,

00:06:16.947 --> 00:06:18.673
and we do talk things over occasionally,

00:06:18.673 --> 00:06:20.330
but we don't talk them over very often,

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:22.932
because I know how he
thinks, he knows how I think.

00:06:22.932 --> 00:06:26.229
He's taught me a lot,
and why should I pay for

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:27.860
a phone call if I know
what he's going to say.

00:06:27.860 --> 00:06:29.292
(audience laughing)

00:06:29.292 --> 00:06:32.548
So if I need to get a cabinet around to

00:06:32.548 --> 00:06:34.574
make decisions or anything like that,

00:06:34.574 --> 00:06:37.499
I probably shouldn't be running the place.

00:06:37.499 --> 00:06:40.951
And that doesn't bother me
to have the responsibility

00:06:40.951 --> 00:06:43.540
for it, but I just don't
want to put it up to a vote.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.149
Because I've seen too many,

00:06:45.149 --> 00:06:47.327
particularly in investment world,

00:06:47.327 --> 00:06:49.290
as you get larger and larger
groups making the decisions,

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:51.583
they get more and more homogenized.

00:06:51.583 --> 00:06:54.179
And I don't think you will ever get

00:06:54.179 --> 00:06:57.136
brilliant investment decisions
out of a large committee.

00:06:57.136 --> 00:07:00.691
I always look at IQ and talent as sort of

00:07:00.691 --> 00:07:04.152
representing the horsepower of the motor.

00:07:04.152 --> 00:07:06.347
But then in terms of the
output, the efficiency

00:07:06.347 --> 00:07:09.344
with which the motor works,
that depends on rationality

00:07:09.344 --> 00:07:11.481
because a lot of people
start off with 400-horsepower

00:07:11.481 --> 00:07:14.135
motors and get 100 horsepower of output.

00:07:14.135 --> 00:07:16.990
And it's way better to
have a 200-horsepower motor

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:18.811
and get it all into output.

00:07:18.811 --> 00:07:23.107
So why do smart people do
things that interfere with

00:07:23.107 --> 00:07:25.639
really getting the output
they're entitled to?

00:07:25.639 --> 00:07:28.139
And it's, it gets into habits,

00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:30.942
into character, into temperament.

00:07:30.942 --> 00:07:33.182
It really gets into behaving
in a rational manner

00:07:33.182 --> 00:07:35.900
and getting in your own way.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:39.191
As I say, everybody here as the ability,

00:07:39.191 --> 00:07:42.558
absolutely, to do anything
I do and much beyond.

00:07:42.558 --> 00:07:45.034
And some of you will
and some of you won't.

00:07:45.034 --> 00:07:48.080
But it will, the ones that won't,

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:51.470
it will be because you
get in your own way.

00:07:51.470 --> 00:07:53.684
It won't be because the
world doesn't allow you to.

00:07:53.684 --> 00:07:56.919
It will be because you
don't allow yourself.

00:07:56.919 --> 00:07:59.377
You will move in the
direction of the people

00:07:59.377 --> 00:08:00.635
that you associate with.

00:08:00.635 --> 00:08:03.179
So it's important to
associate with people that

00:08:03.179 --> 00:08:06.075
are better than yourself, and
actually the most important

00:08:06.075 --> 00:08:07.959
decision many of you make, not all of you,

00:08:07.959 --> 00:08:09.786
will be the spouse you choose.

00:08:09.786 --> 00:08:12.036
And you really, you want to

00:08:14.294 --> 00:08:16.051
associate with people who
are the kind of person

00:08:16.051 --> 00:08:17.263
you'd like to be.

00:08:17.263 --> 00:08:18.919
You'll move in that direction.

00:08:18.919 --> 00:08:21.278
And the most important person, by far,

00:08:21.278 --> 00:08:23.443
in that respect is your spouse.

00:08:23.443 --> 00:08:25.170
I can't overemphasize
how important that is.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:28.420
And you're right, the friends you have,

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:32.342
they will form you as you go through life.

00:08:32.342 --> 00:08:33.175
And...

00:08:35.769 --> 00:08:38.361
make some good friends, keep
them for the rest of your life,

00:08:38.361 --> 00:08:42.528
but have them be people that
you admire as well as like.

00:08:43.618 --> 00:08:46.451
My old fashioned belief that I can

00:08:47.314 --> 00:08:49.828
only should expect to
make money and things

00:08:49.828 --> 00:08:52.152
that I understand, and
when I say understand,

00:08:52.152 --> 00:08:55.631
I don't mean understand
what the product does

00:08:55.631 --> 00:08:57.124
or anything like that.

00:08:57.124 --> 00:08:59.937
I mean understand what the
economics of the business

00:08:59.937 --> 00:09:02.697
are likely to look like 10 years from now

00:09:02.697 --> 00:09:04.347
or 20 years from now.

00:09:04.347 --> 00:09:07.675
I know in general what
the economics of, say,

00:09:07.675 --> 00:09:11.245
Wrigley chewing gum will
look like 10 years from now.

00:09:11.245 --> 00:09:14.304
Internet is going to change
the way people chew gum.

00:09:14.304 --> 00:09:17.325
It isn't going to change
which gum they chew.

00:09:17.325 --> 00:09:21.365
If you own the chewing
gum market in a big way,

00:09:21.365 --> 00:09:23.158
and you've got double mint and spearmint

00:09:23.158 --> 00:09:26.465
and juicy fruit, those
brands will be there

00:09:26.465 --> 00:09:27.570
10 years from now.

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.317
So I can't pinpoint
exactly what the numbers

00:09:30.317 --> 00:09:31.602
are going to look like exactly on Wrigley,

00:09:31.602 --> 00:09:33.200
but I'm not going to
be way off if I try to

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:35.585
look forward on something like that.

00:09:35.585 --> 00:09:37.795
Evaluating that company
is within what I call

00:09:37.795 --> 00:09:39.101
my circle of competence.

00:09:39.101 --> 00:09:41.736
I understand what they do, I understand

00:09:41.736 --> 00:09:43.875
the economics of it, I
understand the competitive

00:09:43.875 --> 00:09:45.601
aspects of the business.

00:09:45.601 --> 00:09:49.345
So figuring out the economic consequences.

00:09:49.345 --> 00:09:52.828
TV: I think there's, I
don't know, 20, 25 million

00:09:52.828 --> 00:09:54.943
sets a year sold in the United States.

00:09:54.943 --> 00:09:55.814
I don't think there's one of them made in

00:09:55.814 --> 00:09:57.725
the United States anymore.

00:09:57.725 --> 00:09:59.091
You'd say, TVs that manufacture,

00:09:59.091 --> 00:10:01.481
what a wonderful business.

00:10:01.481 --> 00:10:03.932
Nobody had a TV in 1950,

00:10:03.932 --> 00:10:05.932
thereabouts, '45 to '50.

00:10:06.995 --> 00:10:09.538
Everybody has multiple sets now.

00:10:09.538 --> 00:10:12.173
Nobody in the United States
has made any real money

00:10:12.173 --> 00:10:14.115
making the sets, they're
all out of business.

00:10:14.115 --> 00:10:17.306
The Magnavoxes, the RCAs,
all of those companies.

00:10:17.306 --> 00:10:20.057
Radio was the equivalent,
over 500 companies

00:10:20.057 --> 00:10:22.043
making radios in the 1920s.

00:10:22.043 --> 00:10:24.915
Again, I don't think there's
a US radio manufacturer

00:10:24.915 --> 00:10:26.356
at the present time.

00:10:26.356 --> 00:10:28.624
But Coca-Cola, you know,

00:10:28.624 --> 00:10:31.294
what was it, 1884, Jacob's
pharmacy, whatever,

00:10:31.294 --> 00:10:33.160
this fellow comes up with something.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:35.776
A lot of copiers over the years,

00:10:35.776 --> 00:10:37.914
but now you've got a company that

00:10:37.914 --> 00:10:42.558
is selling roughly 1.1
billion eight-ounce servings

00:10:42.558 --> 00:10:45.763
of its product, not all
Coke; Sprite and some others,

00:10:45.763 --> 00:10:49.609
daily throughout the
world 117 years later.

00:10:49.609 --> 00:10:53.290
So understanding the
economic characteristics

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:55.701
of a business is different than predicting

00:10:55.701 --> 00:10:58.362
the fact that an industry
is going to do wonderfully.

00:10:58.362 --> 00:11:02.411
So when I look at the Internet business

00:11:02.411 --> 00:11:03.965
and I look at tech (mumbles),

00:11:03.965 --> 00:11:05.420
I say this is a marvelous thing.

00:11:05.420 --> 00:11:07.782
And I love to play
around with the computer,

00:11:07.782 --> 00:11:10.484
I order my books from Amazon
and all kinds of things.

00:11:10.484 --> 00:11:12.327
But I don't know who's going to win.

00:11:12.327 --> 00:11:14.392
Unless I know who's going to win,

00:11:14.392 --> 00:11:15.578
I'm not interested in investing.

00:11:15.578 --> 00:11:17.299
I'll just play around on the computer.

00:11:17.299 --> 00:11:19.206
(audience chuckling)

00:11:19.206 --> 00:11:21.468
Defining your circle of competence

00:11:21.468 --> 00:11:24.634
is the most important aspect of investing.

00:11:24.634 --> 00:11:27.467
It's not how large your circle is.

00:11:28.392 --> 00:11:30.836
You don't have to be an
expert on everything.

00:11:30.836 --> 00:11:33.429
But knowing where the
perimeter of that circle

00:11:33.429 --> 00:11:35.348
of what you know and
what you don't know is

00:11:35.348 --> 00:11:38.916
and staying inside of it is all important.

00:11:38.916 --> 00:11:41.873
Tom Watson Sr. started IBM,

00:11:41.873 --> 00:11:45.367
said in his book, he
said, "I'm no genius,"

00:11:45.367 --> 00:11:47.282
he said, "but I'm smart
in spots and I stay

00:11:47.282 --> 00:11:49.074
"around those spots."

00:11:49.074 --> 00:11:51.274
And that is the key.

00:11:51.274 --> 00:11:53.387
So if I understand a few things,

00:11:53.387 --> 00:11:57.128
and I stick in that arena, I'll do okay.

00:11:57.128 --> 00:11:58.457
And if I don't understand something,

00:11:58.457 --> 00:11:59.867
but I get all excited about it because

00:11:59.867 --> 00:12:00.700
my neighbors are talking about it,

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.696
the stocks are going up, everything,

00:12:02.696 --> 00:12:04.320
I start fooling around someplace else,

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:06.311
eventually I'll get creamed, and I should.

00:12:06.311 --> 00:12:07.394
Well in 1973,

00:12:09.434 --> 00:12:12.634
the Washington Post company
gone public in 1971,

00:12:12.634 --> 00:12:14.395
right about the Pentagon Papers time.

00:12:14.395 --> 00:12:17.478
But in '73, the Nixon Administration,

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:22.467
was, through Bebe Rebozo,
who was a pal of Nixon's,

00:12:22.467 --> 00:12:25.135
they were challenging the licenses

00:12:25.135 --> 00:12:27.918
of two of the Florida television
stations the Post owned.

00:12:27.918 --> 00:12:31.630
So the stock went from 37 down to 16.

00:12:31.630 --> 00:12:33.538
Now at 16, there were about five million

00:12:33.538 --> 00:12:35.765
shares outstanding, so
the whole Washington Post

00:12:35.765 --> 00:12:37.781
company was selling for $80 million.

00:12:37.781 --> 00:12:40.244
And that included the
newspaper, four big TV stations,

00:12:40.244 --> 00:12:42.142
Newsweek, and some other assets,

00:12:42.142 --> 00:12:43.741
and no debt to speak of.

00:12:43.741 --> 00:12:46.225
So the Washington Post company,
which was intrinsically

00:12:46.225 --> 00:12:48.184
worth four or $500 million,

00:12:48.184 --> 00:12:50.527
was selling for about $80
million in the market.

00:12:50.527 --> 00:12:52.235
We bought most of our stock
at about the equivalent

00:12:52.235 --> 00:12:53.762
of $100 million in the market.

00:12:53.762 --> 00:12:55.778
And it was ridiculous.

00:12:55.778 --> 00:12:57.891
You had a business the
unquestionably was worth

00:12:57.891 --> 00:12:59.699
four or five times what
it was selling for.

00:12:59.699 --> 00:13:02.498
And Nixon wasn't going to
put them out of business.

00:13:02.498 --> 00:13:05.369
- When you're doing these
analyzes, then and now,

00:13:05.369 --> 00:13:07.612
do you have computers that help you?

00:13:07.612 --> 00:13:09.289
Or how did you actually read all,

00:13:09.289 --> 00:13:11.258
were you just printed
materials, or how did you,

00:13:11.258 --> 00:13:13.324
in those days, get the materials to read

00:13:13.324 --> 00:13:14.398
about the Washington Post?

00:13:14.398 --> 00:13:15.473
And how do you do it today?

00:13:15.473 --> 00:13:17.580
- Well, pretty much the same way,

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:19.311
except there's fewer opportunities now.

00:13:19.311 --> 00:13:21.733
But I met Bob Woodward back,

00:13:21.733 --> 00:13:25.089
and he'd just come out with
All the President's Men,

00:13:25.089 --> 00:13:27.853
and all of sudden at, oh, 30 yeas of age

00:13:27.853 --> 00:13:29.063
he was getting quite wealthy.

00:13:29.063 --> 00:13:32.980
And we had breakfast or lunch
over at the Madison Hotel.

00:13:32.980 --> 00:13:34.598
He said, what do I do with this money?

00:13:34.598 --> 00:13:37.327
And I said, investing
is just about assigning

00:13:37.327 --> 00:13:38.692
yourself the right story.

00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:41.205
I said, imagine Ben Bradley
this morning said to you,

00:13:41.205 --> 00:13:43.034
what is the Washington Post company worth,

00:13:43.034 --> 00:13:44.041
what would you do,

00:13:44.041 --> 00:13:45.516
if you have to write the story in a month?

00:13:45.516 --> 00:13:47.385
You'd go out and interview TV brokers

00:13:47.385 --> 00:13:49.446
and newspaper brokers and the owners,

00:13:49.446 --> 00:13:51.339
and you try and value each asset.

00:13:51.339 --> 00:13:54.239
I said, that's what I do, I
assign myself the right story.

00:13:54.239 --> 00:13:56.101
And it's nothing more than that.

00:13:56.101 --> 00:13:57.776
Now there's some stories I can't write.

00:13:57.776 --> 00:14:00.946
If you ask me to write a story on whether

00:14:00.946 --> 00:14:04.639
some glamorous, but
non-profit-making business worth,

00:14:04.639 --> 00:14:06.114
I don't know how to write that story.

00:14:06.114 --> 00:14:08.296
But if you ask me to
write a story on what is

00:14:08.296 --> 00:14:10.767
Potomac Electric Power worth
or something like that,

00:14:10.767 --> 00:14:13.279
I can write the story, and
that's what I'm doing everyday.

00:14:13.279 --> 00:14:15.725
I'm assigning myself
and then I go out and--

00:14:15.725 --> 00:14:17.409
One thing I will tell
you that I didn't realize

00:14:17.409 --> 00:14:20.886
when I was getting out
of school is how much

00:14:20.886 --> 00:14:24.219
the unusual person will jump out at you.

00:14:25.974 --> 00:14:28.192
And it isn't because they got 200 IQs

00:14:28.192 --> 00:14:29.074
or anything like that.

00:14:29.074 --> 00:14:31.089
It's really because of
just how they behave,

00:14:31.089 --> 00:14:32.853
what they bring,

00:14:32.853 --> 00:14:35.003
the energy they bring,
the commitment they bring,

00:14:35.003 --> 00:14:37.968
the quality of how they do things,

00:14:37.968 --> 00:14:40.249
how they treat the people around
them, all kinds of things.

00:14:40.249 --> 00:14:44.746
But you will jump out much
more than you might anticipate.

00:14:44.746 --> 00:14:48.163
One of the things you
want to be sure to do

00:14:49.432 --> 00:14:51.755
is, whether you like it or not,

00:14:51.755 --> 00:14:54.757
get very comfortable, it may take a while,

00:14:54.757 --> 00:14:56.426
with public speaking, for example.

00:14:56.426 --> 00:14:58.343
I mean, that's an asset

00:14:59.321 --> 00:15:01.802
that will last you 50 or 60 years,

00:15:01.802 --> 00:15:04.297
and it's a liability if
you don't like doing it

00:15:04.297 --> 00:15:06.698
or uncomfortable doing it,
that also will last you

00:15:06.698 --> 00:15:09.538
50 or 60 yeas, and it's a necessary skill.

00:15:09.538 --> 00:15:12.311
But a lot of those skills
are just human skills.

00:15:12.311 --> 00:15:15.060
If you get the best out
of people around you,

00:15:15.060 --> 00:15:16.347
that's a rare talent.

00:15:16.347 --> 00:15:19.055
And it doesn't correlate with IQ.

00:15:19.055 --> 00:15:22.038
It correlates with an attitude

00:15:22.038 --> 00:15:24.435
toward the world and toward other people.

00:15:24.435 --> 00:15:26.883
It's a talent that, if you work at early

00:15:26.883 --> 00:15:28.353
and you're really conscious about it,

00:15:28.353 --> 00:15:29.944
you don't have to show up other people,

00:15:29.944 --> 00:15:31.426
you don't have to be
smarter than they are.

00:15:31.426 --> 00:15:33.635
You want to bring out the best in them.

00:15:33.635 --> 00:15:35.605
It will pay huge dividends.

00:15:35.605 --> 00:15:39.625
I went one-on-one with
Lebron a few years ago,

00:15:39.625 --> 00:15:41.673
and we had a jump ball.

00:15:41.673 --> 00:15:45.103
And Lebron went up, he got the tip,

00:15:45.103 --> 00:15:47.145
went the length of the court,

00:15:47.145 --> 00:15:48.698
dunked it, and just then turned around,

00:15:48.698 --> 00:15:51.529
and just then I was jumping.
(chuckles)

00:15:51.529 --> 00:15:52.362
I got lucky.

00:15:52.362 --> 00:15:54.142
Bill says if I'd been born
a few thousand years ago,

00:15:54.142 --> 00:15:55.497
I'd have been some animals lunch,

00:15:55.497 --> 00:15:59.437
because I'd have gone on
saying, well I allocate capital.

00:15:59.437 --> 00:16:00.724
The animal would say,
they're the kind that

00:16:00.724 --> 00:16:02.415
taste the best.

00:16:02.415 --> 00:16:04.523
And I can't run fast
and I can't climb trees.

00:16:04.523 --> 00:16:06.163
You don't need a lot of
brains in this business.

00:16:06.163 --> 00:16:07.428
I mean, I've always said, if you got a IQ

00:16:07.428 --> 00:16:09.903
of 160, give away 30
points to somebody else

00:16:09.903 --> 00:16:11.186
'cause you don't need it in investments.

00:16:11.186 --> 00:16:13.311
What you do need is emotional stability.

00:16:13.311 --> 00:16:14.932
I've got a partner (mumbles),

00:16:14.932 --> 00:16:16.481
says he admired humility enormously,

00:16:16.481 --> 00:16:18.827
but he wasn't quite given his fair share.

00:16:18.827 --> 00:16:20.581
(audience laughing)

00:16:20.581 --> 00:16:24.941
We call on people that
have a billion or more,

00:16:24.941 --> 00:16:26.759
at least Steve tells us they do.

00:16:26.759 --> 00:16:28.207
And all we asked is 50%.

00:16:28.207 --> 00:16:29.040
So if...

00:16:31.002 --> 00:16:32.408
If you can't,

00:16:32.408 --> 00:16:33.957
if you have trouble
living on $500 million,

00:16:33.957 --> 00:16:36.106
I'm going to put out a book,
How To Live On $500 million.

00:16:36.106 --> 00:16:37.638
(audience laughing)

00:16:37.638 --> 00:16:39.565
I don't think I can win every game.

00:16:39.565 --> 00:16:40.880
If somebody said how do
you beat Bobby Fisher,

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:43.044
you play him any game except chess.

00:16:43.044 --> 00:16:45.072
So I don't play Bobby Fisher in chess.

00:16:45.072 --> 00:16:46.822
Let's do it here now.

00:16:47.903 --> 00:16:51.608
♫ Now the end is near

00:16:51.608 --> 00:16:55.441
♫ And so I face the final curtain

00:16:55.441 --> 00:17:00.058
♫ My friends, I'll say it clear

00:17:00.058 --> 00:17:01.535
♫ I'll state the (mumbles)

00:17:01.535 --> 00:17:03.870
♫ Something or else on which I'm certain

00:17:03.870 --> 00:17:05.306
(chuckles)

00:17:05.306 --> 00:17:09.020
♫ I've lived the life of spoil

00:17:09.020 --> 00:17:13.431
♫ I've traveled each and every highway

00:17:13.431 --> 00:17:17.074
♫ And more, much more than this

00:17:17.074 --> 00:17:18.946
♫ I did it

00:17:18.946 --> 00:17:21.029
♫ My way

00:17:24.662 --> 00:17:26.159
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:17:26.159 --> 00:17:28.290
I made this video because
a lot of you enjoyed

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:31.332
the Warren Buffet top 10
rules to success volume one.

00:17:31.332 --> 00:17:33.119
So if there someone who you really enjoyed

00:17:33.119 --> 00:17:35.828
the volume one of, and you
want us to do a volume two,

00:17:35.828 --> 00:17:37.444
let me know, let in in the comments below,

00:17:37.444 --> 00:17:38.619
and I'll see what I can do.

00:17:38.619 --> 00:17:40.325
I'd also love to know what did Warren say

00:17:40.325 --> 00:17:42.154
that had the biggest impact on you?

00:17:42.154 --> 00:17:43.713
What lesson are going to take from this

00:17:43.713 --> 00:17:46.811
and immediately apply it to
your life or to your business?

00:17:46.811 --> 00:17:48.145
Leave it in the comments below.

00:17:48.145 --> 00:17:49.445
I'm super curious to find out.

00:17:49.445 --> 00:17:51.667
Finally, I wanted to give a
quick shoutout to Riza Pereira.

00:17:51.667 --> 00:17:53.664
Riza, thank you so much
for picking up a copy

00:17:53.664 --> 00:17:54.907
of my book, Your One Word,

00:17:54.907 --> 00:17:56.965
and sharing that picture with me.

00:17:56.965 --> 00:17:59.126
I really, really, really,
really appreciate it.

00:17:59.126 --> 00:18:01.455
So thank you guys again for
watching. I believe in you.

00:18:01.455 --> 00:18:03.552
I hope you continue to
believe in yourself.

00:18:03.552 --> 00:18:05.628
And whatever your one word is,

00:18:05.628 --> 00:18:08.045
much love, I'll see you soon.

00:18:14.289 --> 00:18:15.976
- The whole long-term capital management,

00:18:15.976 --> 00:18:17.627
and I hope most of you
are familiar with it,

00:18:17.627 --> 00:18:20.575
but the whole story is really fascinating,

00:18:20.575 --> 00:18:24.093
because if you take John Meriwether

00:18:24.093 --> 00:18:28.474
and Eric Rosenfeld, Larry
Hildebrand, Greg Hawkins,

00:18:28.474 --> 00:18:31.667
Victor Agani, the two Nobel Prize winners,

00:18:31.667 --> 00:18:35.519
Murtin Sholes, if you take the 16 of them,

00:18:35.519 --> 00:18:37.455
they probably have as high an average IQ

00:18:37.455 --> 00:18:39.806
as any 16 people working
together in one business

00:18:39.806 --> 00:18:41.630
in the country, including Microsoft

00:18:41.630 --> 00:18:43.527
or wherever you want to name.

00:18:43.527 --> 00:18:47.405
So there's incredible amount
of intellect in that room.

00:18:47.405 --> 00:18:50.397
Now you combine that with the fact

00:18:50.397 --> 00:18:53.561
that those 16 have had
extensive experience

00:18:53.561 --> 00:18:55.152
in the field they are operating in.

00:18:55.152 --> 00:18:57.929
These, was not a bunch of guys who have

00:18:57.929 --> 00:18:59.839
made their money selling men's clothing,

00:18:59.839 --> 00:19:01.810
and then all of a sudden went
into the securities business.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.373
They had,

00:19:03.373 --> 00:19:05.733
they'd had, in aggregate,
the 16 probably had

00:19:05.733 --> 00:19:08.824
350 or 400 years of
experience doing exactly

00:19:08.824 --> 00:19:10.460
what they were doing.

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.918
And then you throw in the third factor.

00:19:12.918 --> 00:19:15.465
That most of them had
virtually all of their

00:19:15.465 --> 00:19:17.478
very substantial net
worths in the business.

00:19:17.478 --> 00:19:19.029
So they had their own money up,

00:19:19.029 --> 00:19:20.550
hundreds and hundreds
of millions of dollars

00:19:20.550 --> 00:19:23.103
of their own money up,
super high intellect,

00:19:23.103 --> 00:19:25.409
working in a field they knew,

00:19:25.409 --> 00:19:28.025
and essentially they went broke.

00:19:28.025 --> 00:19:30.115
And that to me is absolutely fascinating.

00:19:30.115 --> 00:19:32.715
I mean, if I ever write a
book, it's going to be called

00:19:32.715 --> 00:19:35.302
Why Smart People Do Dumb Things.

00:19:35.302 --> 00:19:37.354
My partner says it should
be autobiographical,

00:19:37.354 --> 00:19:39.588
but I--
(audience laughing)

00:19:39.588 --> 00:19:41.590
But this might be an
interesting illustration,

00:19:41.590 --> 00:19:44.413
and these are perfectly decent guys.

00:19:44.413 --> 00:19:45.834
I respect them, and they helped me out

00:19:45.834 --> 00:19:48.066
when I had problems with Solomon.

00:19:48.066 --> 00:19:51.346
So they are not bad people at all.

00:19:51.346 --> 00:19:56.169
But to make money they
didn't have and didn't need,

00:19:56.169 --> 00:19:58.557
they risked what they
did have and did need.

00:19:58.557 --> 00:20:00.369
And that's foolish, that
is just plain foolish.

00:20:00.369 --> 00:20:02.312
It doesn't make a
difference what your IQ is.

00:20:02.312 --> 00:20:05.884
If you risk something
that is important to you

00:20:05.884 --> 00:20:08.374
for something that is unimportant to you,

00:20:08.374 --> 00:20:10.069
it just does not make any sense.

00:20:10.069 --> 00:20:11.858
I don't care whether
the odds are 100 to one

00:20:11.858 --> 00:20:13.912
that you succeed or 1,000
to one that you succeed.

00:20:13.912 --> 00:20:17.297
If you hand me a gun
with a thousand chambers,

00:20:17.297 --> 00:20:19.177
a million chambers in
it, and there's a bullet

00:20:19.177 --> 00:20:20.890
in one chamber and you
said, put it up your temple,

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:24.175
how much do you want to
be paid to pull it once,

00:20:24.175 --> 00:20:25.476
I'm not going to pull it.

00:20:25.476 --> 00:20:27.437
You can name any sum you
want, but it doesn't do

00:20:27.437 --> 00:20:28.589
anything for me.

00:20:28.589 --> 00:20:31.568
On the upside, and I think
the downside's fairly clear.

00:20:31.568 --> 00:20:33.152
(audience laughing)

00:20:33.152 --> 00:20:35.360
So I'm not interested
in that kind of a game,

00:20:35.360 --> 00:20:38.326
and yet people do it financially without

00:20:38.326 --> 00:20:39.835
thinking about it very much.

00:20:39.835 --> 00:20:42.332
Generally speaking, investing in yourself

00:20:42.332 --> 00:20:43.601
is the best thing you can do,

00:20:43.601 --> 00:20:44.800
anything that improves your own talents.

00:20:44.800 --> 00:20:46.416
Nobody can take it away from me.

00:20:46.416 --> 00:20:48.479
They can run up huge
deficits and the dollar

00:20:48.479 --> 00:20:50.448
can become worth far less,

00:20:50.448 --> 00:20:52.165
you can have all kinds of things happen,

00:20:52.165 --> 00:20:54.203
but if you've got talent yourself

00:20:54.203 --> 00:20:56.346
and you've maximized your talent,

00:20:56.346 --> 00:20:58.331
you've got a terrific asset.

00:20:58.331 --> 00:20:59.879
So that doesn't mean everybody
should go to college,

00:20:59.879 --> 00:21:03.135
but it does mean that any
way you find to improve.

00:21:03.135 --> 00:21:05.255
Communication skills are
enormously important.

00:21:05.255 --> 00:21:07.987
I took a Dale Carnegie
course that I paid $100 for,

00:21:07.987 --> 00:21:10.401
and it was worth a college degree.

00:21:10.401 --> 00:21:12.835
At least, I thought it was. (laughing)

00:21:12.835 --> 00:21:14.772
Maybe this interview will
convince people otherwise.

